#!/usr/bin/env bash
# kit-changes - lists each open change's reviews and checks, and the changes merged last (kit verb: workflow)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kit changes [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# Draws what the forge says of the repository's changes, a stage for each of Changes' tabs in kittui that the forge
# answers. It changes nothing. The tab changes is kit flow's, as kit flow changes prints it. The stages, in order:
#
#   reviews → checks → merged
#
# kit --workspace=<workspace> changes, the kit option before the verb, draws each stage across a workspace's members
# with checkouts, as kit workspace does: a group's name, or a repository's host and path, as kit workspaces names it;
# none names no workspace. Without it, the workspace is the repository here, and outside one, the last group used, as
# kit workspaces --current says. On a terminal, kit changes opens Changes in kittui, and kit changes <stage> opens it on
# that tab, maximized; so does kit changes changes. Without one, or with --text, it prints the stages as text; with
# --json or --jq, as JSON; the tab of kit flow, with a form, is refused, naming kit flow.
#
#   --text              prints the stages as text, even on a terminal
#   --json              the stages as one JSON object: stages, then each stage's document under its name, null for one
#                       that could not be read
#   --jq                the same JSON, formatted by jq for a person
#   --stages            prints the stages, one per line, in order, the same over any workspace
#   --marks             prints a mark per stage, as one JSON object, which changes when what the stage draws does:
#                       for each, the forge's own marks of its issues, which move with its changes, and of its runs
#   reviews             each open change's reviewers: those it asks, and those who reviewed it, by login
#   checks              each open change's checks: the latest run of each name on its head commit
#   merged              the changes merged last, the 50 newest, merged last first
#   changes             a tab kit flow prints; on a terminal it opens in kittui, and with a form it is refused
#
# kit changes --stages
#
# kit changes --marks
#
# kit changes reviews [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit changes checks [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit changes merged [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit changes changes [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# With --json, a stage prints {rows, errors}, and limited where the forge holds more than it read. A row is {key, …,
# links}: key is the row's own in its stage, and links gives where a value leads, by its field. Over a workspace, each
# row gains member, its host and path, and a member that could not be read is {member, error} under errors, while
# every other member still draws. A repository with no forge, or a forge without the stage's feature, gives no rows
# and no errors. reviews and checks read the open changes as kit flow changes does, in one read of the forge a minute.
#
#   reviews             {change, title, login, state, at, unresolved}, keyed by the change's number and the login:
#                       state is requested while the reviewer is asked and has not answered, else approved, changes,
#                       or commented; at is when they answered, in seconds since the epoch, null while asked and on
#                       Azure DevOps, whose votes have no time; unresolved counts the change's unresolved threads. The
#                       author's own comments are no review
#   checks              {change, title, name, verdict, at, took}, keyed by the change's number and the check's name:
#                       verdict is passed, failed, or pending; at is when the run started, in seconds since the epoch,
#                       or null; took is how long it ran, in seconds, null while it runs
#   merged              {number, title, author, branch, merged, by}, keyed by its number: merged is when, in seconds
#                       since the epoch, and by who merged it; the title links to its page. Past 50, the document is
#                       limited at 50
#
# Exits 0 when the stages are drawn, 1 when a stage could not be read, after what was, and 2 on a usage error, or
# outside a repository's working tree with no workspace.
#
# see: modules/bash/apps.sh, modules/bash/apps/changes.sh, modules/jq/apps.jq, docs/apps.md

set -o errexit -o nounset -o pipefail
# shellcheck source=modules/bash/1st.sh
source "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/../modules/bash/1st.sh"
kit:enter
use apps
workspaces:misplaced changes "$@" || exit $?
guard:arguments changes "$@" || exit $?
apps:main changes "$@"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
